/**

@page page_exahype_domain-decomposition Domain decomposition

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE uses Peano's deterministic rank ownership and subtree-task traversal.
@ref page_peano_domain_decomposition describes the tree, pools, boundary
exchange, and regrid migration. @ref page_peano_runtime_analysis describes the
reported balance metrics.

# Solver data

Each MPI rank owns its solver repository and the rank-local cell, face, and
vertex pools. Generated observers translate traversal pool slots into solver
data references. Cells belong to one rank. Faces and vertices on a rank
boundary have local copies that are merged with the remote frozen state before
their first consuming event.

Subtree tasks use observer clones and task-local copies of the interface
entities. The generated merge routines receive `InterRank` or `IntraRank` so a
data model can apply the appropriate exchange policy.

# Solver state

All observer clones on one rank refer to the same persistent solver instances.
A mutable solver attribute accessed from cell callbacks therefore requires a
thread-safe update. A scalable reduction lets each observer clone accumulate
private state and combines it once from `endTraversal()`.

Attributes that must agree across ranks require an explicit MPI collective at
a point reached by every rank in the same program step. The tarch MPI wrappers
provide reductions and broadcasts with the runtime's timeout and progress
handling.

ExaHyPE applies this pattern to admissible time-step sizes, mesh-size bounds,
and other solver-wide values. An override of a solver lifecycle routine calls
its base implementation so these reductions remain active.

# Adaptive ownership

ExaHyPE commits cell-based refine and erase events through
exahype2::RefinementControlService. The service gives every rank the same
canonical request set. Peano derives the target tree and leaf-weighted rank
ownership from that set and migrates solver pools as part of the transition.

The geometric partition uses leaf counts. Particle-heavy cells, limiter work,
or heterogeneous kernels can therefore produce application load imbalance on
a geometrically balanced mesh. Solver tasking and parallel kernels provide the
additional concurrency for these cases.

*/
